      { "speaker": "Waiter", "native": "கொஞ்சம் காரம் இருக்கும் சார்.", "roman": "konjam kāram irukkum sār.", "en": "A little spicy, sir." },
      { "speaker": "Tom", "native": "பரவால்ல. எனக்குக் காரம் பிடிக்கும்!", "roman": "paravālla. enakkuk kāram piḍikkum!", "en": "That's fine. I like spicy food!" },
      { "speaker": "Karthik", "native": "அப்புறம் ரெண்டு ஃபில்டர் காபி.", "roman": "appuram reṇḍu filṭar kāpi.", "en": "And then two filter coffees." },
      { "speaker": "Tom", "native": "ரொம்ப ருசியா இருக்கு! இன்னும் கொஞ்சம் சாம்பார் குடுங்க.", "roman": "romba rusiyā irukku! innum konjam sāmbār kuḍunga.", "en": "It's delicious! A little more sambar, please." },
      { "speaker": "Karthik", "native": "சாப்பிட்டாச்சா? பில் குடுங்க!", "roman": "sāppiṭṭācchā? bil kuḍunga!", "en": "Finished eating? The bill, please!" }
    ]
  },
  "culture": "Traditional meals are eaten with the right hand, often from a banana leaf (வாழை இலை). Hosts will keep serving you; say போதும் 'enough' firmly — and folding the leaf towards you signals you have finished and enjoyed the meal. Filter coffee is served in a tumbler and dabarah (டபரா) and 'pulled' between the two to cool and froth it. Many Tamil homes and restaurants are vegetarian (சைவம்); a 'military hotel' is, oddly, a non-vegetarian restaurant.",
  "lessons": [
    {
